WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.410
&gt;&gt; The Audit Committee meets pretty regularly.

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:16.010
It has a full programme of work, as you can
see from the minutes of the last meeting.

00:00:16.010 --> 00:00:21.900
The points that I want to draw to the Board's
attention are firstly the construction of

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:28.970
an issues list, which I regard as a principal
way of identifying those high-risk and urgent

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:36.570
matters that need to be brought to the Board's
attention and where management are focused

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:40.980
on essentially executing against them in pretty
short order.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:47.640
I hope that list does not grow and grow; I
hope it is a dynamic list that has about half

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:52.930
a dozen items on it and that items move off
that list, and new ones will inevitably come

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:56.930
in through the course of time.

00:00:56.930 --> 00:01:03.860
I hope, again, that the Board will focus and
ask questions on that over time.

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:08.350
The second point is just to reaffirm the Board
Assurance Framework and its importance in

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:12.610
driving risk management and risk assurance.

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:18.770
Thirdly, to note that the internal auditors
now have a full programme of work.

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:24.200
We appointed Deloitte a little while ago,
and their work is progressing across a broad

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:32.480
front and will be an essential piece of the
assurance mechanisms to the Board.

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:40.940
Fourthly, whilst you went past the last set
of accounts pretty fast, next year's set of

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:46.060
accounts will be rather more complicated inasmuch
as it will include the consolidation of all

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:52.330
of the CCGs and the accountability for the
£96.5 billion of funds.

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:54.829
That is no small task.

00:01:54.829 --> 00:01:57.420
The planning needs to start now; it has started
now.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:59.430
A number of dry runs will be needed.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:05.430
It will not just be numbers; it will be governance
statements and a whole host of things.

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:11.440
Whilst it is only an annual report, I can
assure you that does not happen without a

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:14.500
great deal of effort from across the organisation.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:21.030
Also, just to highlight the risk that the
cut-over from the old system to the new system

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:26.750
is still being worked through in terms of
any liabilities that might come across that

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:28.360
we are unaware of.

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:33.120
Paul will report on that probably later in
the year once that has been worked through

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:35.590
with the department.

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:39.980
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:42.269
May we receive the report from the Audit Committee?

00:02:42.269 --> 00:02:43.370
Thank you very much.

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:52.561
&gt;&gt; I do not think I have anything to add to
this fairly short summary, but Ros will be

00:02:52.561 --> 00:02:55.670
very happy to answer any questions that the
Board has.

00:02:55.670 --> 00:03:03.819
&gt;&gt; I am taking questions for Ros, but I do
not see any.

00:03:03.819 --> 00:03:08.760
Thank you very much, Moira, for the report.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:12.430
&gt;&gt; I can keep this very short and just make
the link to Ciarán's point: this is the point

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:18.720
at which the Arm's-Length Body controls that
you talk about meet the real world of our

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:23.159
operation, and I suppose the operation of
this committee is about the only thing that

00:03:23.159 --> 00:03:25.629
makes it possible to continue to do business.

00:03:25.629 --> 00:03:29.569
I think it holds the distinction of being
the busiest Board sub-committee in the sense

00:03:29.569 --> 00:03:34.480
that it meets weekly and has great excitement
with cases coming through.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.310
Quite how sustainable that is in the long
term, I think I will just have to put as a

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:43.209
matter of some concern, because I suspect
we are still seeing a build-up of cases rather

00:03:43.209 --> 00:03:45.680
than a full caseload, as we go through.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:49.909
It comes back to your point: we do need to
move forward from this, but I think to a large

00:03:49.909 --> 00:03:54.069
extent we are going to have to earn the autonomy
through the way in which we manage these things,

00:03:54.069 --> 00:03:59.470
then I think we will get slightly more release
from these controls.

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:01.550
We are continuing on that front.

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:05.709
Beyond putting on record what we have done,
how many we have approved and how many we

00:04:05.709 --> 00:04:10.930
have had conditions around, etc, I do not
think I have anything more to add to what

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:11.930
has been said.

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:17.019
We will add for future reports the financial
value of the transactions we have put through,

00:04:17.019 --> 00:04:23.360
because that will be more helpful than just
the number of items.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:31.690
&gt;&gt; That 
would be helpful; thank you for that.

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:36.590
&gt;&gt; Again, not much to add to what is here.

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:42.759
The Committee has met twice and has another
meeting planned very shortly.

00:04:42.759 --> 00:04:52.230
Again, judging how much work and how to get
that right, timing, etc, will be important.

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:59.870
Hopefully, the report gives the Board a brief
summary of the work to date.

00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:09.990
&gt;&gt; I 
think what we should just observe is that

00:05:09.990 --> 00:05:14.680
much of the Board's work is done in committees
-- and has to be, because of the volume of

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:20.360
work that is flooding through -- and to thank
my fellow Non-Executive Directors for the

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:24.440
huge amount of time that they put into these
committees and working very closely with the

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:25.470
Executive Team.

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:31.419
It is appropriate that at these full Board
meetings we note summaries of work that has

00:05:31.419 --> 00:05:36.270
been underway rather than re-work the work
of our committees.

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:39.770
That is an important part of the assurance
of the business of the Board as a whole, that

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:41.919
we do as we have just done.

00:05:41.919 --> 00:05:59.770
Thank you for those reports.

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:06.100
&gt;&gt; The Board will recall that the mandate
is the way the government sets out its expectations

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:13.180
for NHS England, and the first mandate was
published last November but only really came

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:17.569
into operation as we did, in April.

00:06:17.569 --> 00:06:24.010
Therefore, this refresh of the first mandate
is pretty early in its life.

00:06:24.010 --> 00:06:31.180
What we are bringing to the Board today is
a report that the government has started its

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:38.620
consultation on refreshing the mandate; it
published that on 5th July.

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:45.430
I think the first point that is worth making
is that, of course, we have not as a Board

00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:51.949
felt that our ambitions should be constrained,
if you like, just by the mandate.

00:06:51.949 --> 00:06:58.280
In our business planning, I think we decided
as a Board that we should go significantly

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:01.320
further in a whole set of areas.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:09.889
Particularly, I think, we anticipated some
of the work of the Francis Report and around

00:07:09.889 --> 00:07:15.240
areas of patient safety and culture, with
Jane's lead on the Six Cs.

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:20.880
I think we made steps well beyond what was
in the first mandate and, indeed, in much

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:27.240
of the area of transparency and public engagement
likewise.

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:45.520
I think that is probably quite important for
us to note.

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:54.879
There are a whole set of proposed changes
to objectives.

00:07:54.879 --> 00:08:03.280
Many of them in their own right are perfectly
good things, and indeed, you could get quite

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:09.550
enthusiastic about a number of the individual
aspirations.

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:20.440
What I think we as a Board will want to think
about when we consider our response is whether

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:29.349
they move in some cases into a world of beginning
to prescribe more process than outcome.

00:08:29.349 --> 00:08:39.169
Of course, the way we have been established,
the way the new health system is set up, is

00:08:39.169 --> 00:08:44.690
on the basis of setting really challenging,
aspirational outcomes, which then, with the

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:51.160
right degree of clinical leadership locally,
the right sort of headroom for really strong

00:08:51.160 --> 00:09:00.100
partnerships to develop locally, those outcomes
will be delivered and indeed exceeded through

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:07.650
the best means available that connect really
well with the people that are being served

00:09:07.650 --> 00:09:08.650
locally.

00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:18.300
My first question would be, are we satisfied
that we can keep hold of that really important

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:23.410
part of our mission, which is to achieve outcomes
through developing the right relationships,

00:09:23.410 --> 00:09:27.930
giving the right degree of clinical freedom?

00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:38.029
The 

00:09:38.029 --> 00:09:44.160
government have been very straightforward
in the mandate consultation by saying that

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:51.160
a number of these proposals could carry additional
cost, but at this stage, they have not been

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:55.450
able to calculate what those costs might be.

00:09:55.450 --> 00:10:04.740
They do make the commitment that when they
do that, they would then need to consider

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:06.820
different priorities.

00:10:06.820 --> 00:10:14.870
We think it is quite important that discipline
of financial rigour -- particularly in the

00:10:14.870 --> 00:10:22.950
financial environment we are working in, which
is pretty tight -- is kept to, or else, completely

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:30.420
unintentionally, we could find that we are
overloading some of the expectations on the

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:36.180
service, which, as Barbara was saying earlier
in the report on performance, can be running

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:38.010
pretty hot at times.

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:46.140
Those would be my two cautionary questions;
a degree of outcomes focus and the right clinical

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:51.730
leadership motivation out there, and keeping
discipline of costs.

00:10:51.730 --> 00:10:58.160
With that, Chairman, I welcome initial comments
from the Board, which then, of course, over

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:03.440
the next months, we would turn into a more
formal response.

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:04.440
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much for that, Bill.

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:09.810
I think it is worth reminding ourselves of
the function of the mandate.

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:17.769
It is a part of the constitutional relationship
between the Board and the government, which

00:11:17.769 --> 00:11:23.790
is established in the 2012 legislation as
resting on three principal pillars.

00:11:23.790 --> 00:11:30.430
One is the Act of Parliament itself, which
requires both the Secretary of State and ourselves

00:11:30.430 --> 00:11:35.519
to share responsibility for the promotion
of a comprehensive National Health Service,

00:11:35.519 --> 00:11:42.850
which requires us both to respect the principles
of autonomy and to drive continuous improvement.

00:11:42.850 --> 00:11:49.510
There is the legislation, but then, secondly,
the NHS Constitution, because the legalisation

00:11:49.510 --> 00:11:55.240
requires us to advance the principles of the
NHS Constitution, and Barbara has described

00:11:55.240 --> 00:12:01.930
to us this morning how that is captured, how
we do it and how we measure our progress.

00:12:01.930 --> 00:12:07.731
The third aspect of the relationship is the
mandate itself, and everybody at the Board

00:12:07.731 --> 00:12:13.420
will recall that when we were consulted last
year on the original mandate, we had I think

00:12:13.420 --> 00:12:19.579
a very constructive discussion with the department
about how the mandate might observe those

00:12:19.579 --> 00:12:25.810
principles of autonomy and of liberating the
NHS, which were, after all, fundamental principles

00:12:25.810 --> 00:12:27.230
of the legislation.

00:12:27.230 --> 00:12:32.029
They are principles that apply not only to
us, but also to the Clinical Commissioning

00:12:32.029 --> 00:12:33.040
Groups.

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:39.310
Our responsibility with them is not to impose
requirements upon them, but to work closely

00:12:39.310 --> 00:12:44.510
with them, to facilitate them and to allow
them to be as good as they possibly can be

00:12:44.510 --> 00:12:49.070
by aligning financial resource with clinical
leadership and clinical judgement.

00:12:49.070 --> 00:12:57.940
That, to me, captures what the model of the
reformed NHS commissioning system involves.

00:12:57.940 --> 00:13:02.180
I think it is rather important that as we
carry forward further discussions with the

00:13:02.180 --> 00:13:10.420
department around the refresh of the mandate,
we continue to respect that legal set of relationships

00:13:10.420 --> 00:13:17.560
and, I would say, to help the government maintain
its vision for the liberated NHS against all

00:13:17.560 --> 00:13:20.649
the other pressures that inevitably are on
the government.

00:13:20.649 --> 00:13:25.820
I think we need to work constructively with
them on that front.

00:13:25.820 --> 00:13:32.260
With that in mind, I would suggest that this
draft refresh of the mandate is exactly that;

00:13:32.260 --> 00:13:35.040
it is a draft that is out for consultation.

00:13:35.040 --> 00:13:41.760
This is genuinely a work in progress and an
area where I think we would look to work constructively

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:43.500
with the government.

00:13:43.500 --> 00:13:53.190
If I have an overriding concern, it is that
we risk the loss of clarity, and without clarity,

00:13:53.190 --> 00:13:54.390
accountability becomes blurred.

00:13:54.390 --> 00:14:02.070
It is absolutely essential that we as a Board
understand that we are legally responsible

00:14:02.070 --> 00:14:04.370
for reporting against the mandate.

00:14:04.370 --> 00:14:10.160
We have an obligation to comply with those
aspects of it that are specified as requirements,

00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:15.329
and we have to seek to comply with those that
are specified as objectives.

00:14:15.329 --> 00:14:20.380
Actually, this is very serious, and your point
about cost is absolutely critical in all of

00:14:20.380 --> 00:14:21.380
this.

00:14:21.380 --> 00:14:28.130
We need accountability, transparency and proper
costing to understand how we would work.

00:14:28.130 --> 00:14:33.501
That point about cost is of course built into
the legislation, so that the mandate itself,

00:14:33.501 --> 00:14:40.199
when it comes formally to be agreed, must
contain a statement of the cost so that it

00:14:40.199 --> 00:14:46.230
is transparent: what are the obligations that
the government is transferring to the NHS

00:14:46.230 --> 00:14:51.380
England Board, and what is the cost associated
with them.

00:14:51.380 --> 00:14:59.540
With all of that, I would hope that this morning
we can consider the principles of the proposed

00:14:59.540 --> 00:15:05.269
update on the mandate, understanding that
we are just 15 weeks into the operation of

00:15:05.269 --> 00:15:13.300
the first iteration of the mandate, and understand
what it is that the government seeks to do

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:19.889
to amend the mandate with effect from 1st
April next year of what is after all a two-year

00:15:19.889 --> 00:15:23.680
mandate established by them in the first instance.

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:29.720
I would hope that throughout all of this,
we can come to an agreement around the nature

00:15:29.720 --> 00:15:36.089
of the mandate that allows us to continue
to pursue our broad ambitions for the future

00:15:36.089 --> 00:15:41.650
of the NHS, and at the same time, to meet
the government's objectives for the NHS, led

00:15:41.650 --> 00:15:47.380
by the Prime Minister and the Health Secretary,
both through the short term and the medium

00:15:47.380 --> 00:15:51.380
term, but also, of course, into the longer
term.

00:15:51.380 --> 00:16:01.759
&gt;&gt; I think Bill's paper is very clear and
very good.

00:16:01.759 --> 00:16:10.880
I would partly agree with your remarks, Chair;
there is the question of the role of NHS England

00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:15.509
and the implications of the mandate refresh
for our role.

00:16:15.509 --> 00:16:19.639
There are two items in particular.

00:16:19.639 --> 00:16:29.300
I do not want to be too specific, but in item
70, the reference to the collection of costs

00:16:29.300 --> 00:16:35.959
from overseas visitors, I think I would welcome
more clarity in the discussions that we have

00:16:35.959 --> 00:16:42.080
with the Secretary of State, first of all
as to the implications of that for our role,

00:16:42.080 --> 00:16:52.339
and the implications of that in public health
terms and in terms of our values, which are

00:16:52.339 --> 00:16:53.339
also included.

00:16:53.339 --> 00:16:59.760
There are implications for our role that I
think we do need clarification on, actually

00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:01.710
throughout this document.

00:17:01.710 --> 00:17:13.510
I would also welcome, I guess, item 27's specific
reference to mental health and emergency crisis

00:17:13.510 --> 00:17:19.289
responses, which I would welcome as a general
point, but again, we need to understand the

00:17:19.289 --> 00:17:24.990
implications for our role and our relationship
with other bodies like the police and social

00:17:24.990 --> 00:17:25.990
services.

00:17:25.990 --> 00:17:30.640
There is a lot of clarification to be done
in moving forward in this document, and there

00:17:30.640 --> 00:17:39.489
are some significant implications for what
NHS England is and what it should be doing.

00:17:39.489 --> 00:17:46.960
&gt;&gt; I am a fan of the mandate document as it
exists, I think.

00:17:46.960 --> 00:17:48.910
I love the focus on the five domains.

00:17:48.910 --> 00:17:54.740
When I was reading this, I was trying to think
through, saying, do these suggestions drive

00:17:54.740 --> 00:17:58.660
the five domains and drive the outcomes?

00:17:58.660 --> 00:18:02.660
A bit like Bill said, there are one or two
things that I said, 'That is exciting, that

00:18:02.660 --> 00:18:05.559
could be fun and could really have an impact.'

00:18:05.559 --> 00:18:14.960
I guess my overriding feeling was that there
was a risk that we were straying into the

00:18:14.960 --> 00:18:16.510
territory of how to do things.

00:18:16.510 --> 00:18:22.440
There is one phrase about, 'We work on CCG
planning guidance with NHS England.'

00:18:22.440 --> 00:18:27.990
That feels unstrategic and not outcome-focused.

00:18:27.990 --> 00:18:31.600
Then I worry that some of the things we are
saying are, 'We will also implement the recommendations

00:18:31.600 --> 00:18:34.610
of this report and put them in the mandate.'

00:18:34.610 --> 00:18:39.530
Maybe it is back to your clarity point, Malcolm,
but the thing I loved about the mandate originally

00:18:39.530 --> 00:18:46.530
was there was a minimal amount of complex,
how, 'processy' stuff around us.

00:18:46.530 --> 00:18:49.270
It was around five domains.

00:18:49.270 --> 00:18:55.690
I would be wanting us to go into the consultation
and the debate around what is in the refresh

00:18:55.690 --> 00:19:00.420
saying, anything that will drive outcomes
harder and sets higher expectations might

00:19:00.420 --> 00:19:07.340
be one thing; anything that begins to dilute
that relentless focus on outcomes I would

00:19:07.340 --> 00:19:09.240
be pushing back on.

00:19:09.240 --> 00:19:11.840
Personal opinion.

00:19:11.840 --> 00:19:20.270
&gt;&gt; I would just echo a number of the comments
that you made in your introduction about how

00:19:20.270 --> 00:19:25.580
one can deal with some of these issues where
they are not very specific.

00:19:25.580 --> 00:19:32.190
When we come back to being accountable against
the mandate, specificity is very important,

00:19:32.190 --> 00:19:40.530
and I think it is very difficult to go out
to consultation to the public without an indication

00:19:40.530 --> 00:19:42.340
of cost on things.

00:19:42.340 --> 00:19:50.130
If one asks whether you would like more of
a certain type of service without any indication

00:19:50.130 --> 00:20:05.470
of how it is going to be, not having the costings
makes it very difficult.

00:20:05.470 --> 00:20:15.120
On Victor's question mark about what is relevant
to the role of NHS England, I think paragraph

00:20:15.120 --> 00:20:21.940
58, which is about additional leadership being
required, is one that does require clarification.

00:20:21.940 --> 00:20:26.870
It is very vague; it is hard to know what
that means.

00:20:26.870 --> 00:20:32.370
&gt;&gt; This is a question, really.

00:20:32.370 --> 00:20:34.910
Obviously this is the first time.

00:20:34.910 --> 00:20:41.880
As with so many things, with a new organisation,
a new system, this is the first time we are

00:20:41.880 --> 00:20:46.679
talking about a refresh of the mandate.

00:20:46.679 --> 00:20:52.640
We are one party to the mandate, as it were,
but we are looking here at a public consultation

00:20:52.640 --> 00:20:54.550
document.

00:20:54.550 --> 00:21:02.080
I suppose my question is, do we not have a
role in the consultation in the sense that

00:21:02.080 --> 00:21:05.190
we are the other side, or do we wait until
the consultation comes to an end, the Department

00:21:05.190 --> 00:21:07.950
of Health takes a view about that consultation
and comes back?

00:21:07.950 --> 00:21:13.340
There has to be some kind of negotiation,
because it cannot simply be an imposition

00:21:13.340 --> 00:21:20.940
of the mandate, particularly around the point
that people have made about costs and deprioritisation

00:21:20.940 --> 00:21:24.300
that is suggested here.

00:21:24.300 --> 00:21:31.100
All of that is quite a long timeline, it seems
to me; again, in terms of the commitment that

00:21:31.100 --> 00:21:40.400
they make in the consultation document about
being committed to providing constancy of

00:21:40.400 --> 00:21:48.320
purpose to enable the NHS plan to go ahead,
the period of preparation, as it were, for

00:21:48.320 --> 00:21:51.170
an altered mandate is not very long.

00:21:51.170 --> 00:21:57.250
I am just interested in Bill's view about
what that process will be, because obviously

00:21:57.250 --> 00:22:04.799
we are also inventing a system that presumably
will be the future pattern, and it feels to

00:22:04.799 --> 00:22:12.550
me that it is very time-consuming, because
we are one party to it, but we have lots and

00:22:12.550 --> 00:22:21.590
lots of CCGs who have expectations of us,
so it is quite tricky.

00:22:21.590 --> 00:22:27.570
&gt;&gt; The government is formally required to
consult on the mandate just with us, of course,

00:22:27.570 --> 00:22:36.710
and this is part of their broader, healthy,
I think, decision to have a wider engagement

00:22:36.710 --> 00:22:41.080
with all interested parties.

00:22:41.080 --> 00:22:46.360
On the back of that, we would expect then
to have some formal engagement on the proposed

00:22:46.360 --> 00:22:50.240
changes to the mandate.

00:22:50.240 --> 00:22:57.250
Negotiation, requirement to agree; no, they
are required to consult.

00:22:57.250 --> 00:23:04.950
Our experience last time was that those were
very open and good discussions, so I go into

00:23:04.950 --> 00:23:13.750
it with some confidence that the government
will want to engage properly with us on this.

00:23:13.750 --> 00:23:19.190
I think your point about constancy of purpose
is really well made.

00:23:19.190 --> 00:23:27.020
What we know is that in the existing mandate,
many of the most important objectives around

00:23:27.020 --> 00:23:33.980
improving outcomes take a period of time to
deliver, and they require us to retain our

00:23:33.980 --> 00:23:43.510
focus, to really work hard on the innovations
that will help bring about a much better understanding,

00:23:43.510 --> 00:23:50.880
new ways to bring about improvements in care,
the whole area of responsiveness to patients,

00:23:50.880 --> 00:23:55.600
which we are opening up through Jane's and
Tim's work.

00:23:55.600 --> 00:24:02.169
All of that requires real commitment over
a period of time, and of course, in our business

00:24:02.169 --> 00:24:05.030
plan, we are setting that out for a three-year
period.

00:24:05.030 --> 00:24:13.110
I really do hope that at the end of this,
we will still be focusing as much of our efforts

00:24:13.110 --> 00:24:19.100
as possible on that mission to improve outcomes
and tackle inequalities right the way across

00:24:19.100 --> 00:24:20.130
the country.

00:24:20.130 --> 00:24:26.370
Where we need to make some adjustments because
it will better help us achieve that in our

00:24:26.370 --> 00:24:32.650
business planning process, reflecting any
amendments to the mandate, then that is great.

00:24:32.650 --> 00:24:41.820
Anything that might risk diverting us or looking
at different matters, I just do not think

00:24:41.820 --> 00:24:47.940
will sit with that absolute core mission to
serve the public and patients by helping improving

00:24:47.940 --> 00:24:52.330
outcomes and tackling and addressing inequalities
where we see it.

00:24:52.330 --> 00:24:59.800
&gt;&gt; I think an answer to Moira's first question
is that, consistent with our values and our

00:24:59.800 --> 00:25:05.340
transparency, we will reply in a formal way,
in the public domain, to the consultation.

00:25:05.340 --> 00:25:09.980
We would expect of course our Executive Team
to be in constant communication with the department,

00:25:09.980 --> 00:25:14.010
but I think it is right that the views that
we have expressed around the table this morning

00:25:14.010 --> 00:25:19.250
are properly recorded and properly reflected.

00:25:19.250 --> 00:25:27.720
&gt;&gt; I think this goes quite well with the next
item, as well, because a proper financial

00:25:27.720 --> 00:25:34.559
evaluation of the outcome of this has not
just one-year impacts, but multiple-year impact.

00:25:34.559 --> 00:25:39.960
It is not just a money thing; it is actually
a capability and capacity thing as well, and

00:25:39.960 --> 00:25:47.559
where I have seen organisations fail is even
if they try to do things more efficiently

00:25:47.559 --> 00:25:48.700
with money.

00:25:48.700 --> 00:25:56.970
It is the amount of intellectual capacity
and time in the system that often becomes

00:25:56.970 --> 00:25:58.270
the break.

00:25:58.270 --> 00:26:06.520
I think we need to evaluate both of those
aspects as we respond to this.

00:26:06.520 --> 00:26:08.390
&gt;&gt; Thank you; that has been a very valuable
discussion.

00:26:08.390 --> 00:26:14.460
I see one of the functions of the mandate
as being of communication.

00:26:14.460 --> 00:26:20.270
It is actually setting out in a formal document
what the government's expectations are of

00:26:20.270 --> 00:26:27.620
the National Health Service.

00:26:27.620 --> 00:26:40.220
There is a tendency that we may observe from
time to time within the NHS to drown clear

00:26:40.220 --> 00:26:47.090
messages.

00:26:47.090 --> 00:26:59.080
That is not at all to dilute what is here
except in terms of volume, but much more to

00:26:59.080 --> 00:27:20.070
focus it and to concentrate it so that we
are on the same page, we understand what 

00:27:20.070 --> 00:28:09.809
is in the mandate.

